**Step 4. Seed from `BACKLOG.md`, OPEN rows only.**
1. **Classify every row by program** (print the table `row#, state word(s), class` into the seed log; STATUS carries the counts). The State cell's leading words decide. SEED: OPEN, DEFERRED, KAM'S CALL, READY FOR REVIEW, BLOCKED, DRAFT, ASSIGNED (case-insensitive, bold/strike markup stripped). NOT SEEDED: MERGED, DEPLOYED, FIXED, DONE, CLOSED, SUPERSEDED, RULED, COVERED, DOCUMENTED, anything struck through (`~~`), and any row whose State opens with a closed word even when it later says "Was: OPEN". **Ambiguous** (a State whose opening word is none of the above, or an OPEN row that also carries "Ruled <date> (C-71 ..." text that may close it): do NOT guess and do NOT seed; list the row number and its first 120 characters under `AMBIGUOUS` in STATUS for Tuesday. Row `#4` is the Jira row itself: not seeded; it is updated in Step 5. Controls that can fail: the classifier run on a scratch table holding one row of each word classifies each as expected, and a planted row `| 999 | x | NOPE |` lands in AMBIGUOUS. **Size guard:** if SEED is more than 100 rows, STOP and ask (`STOPPED: NEEDS TUESDAY <n> open rows, seed all or the first 100?`).
2. **Epics.** Only if the SEED rows clearly group: for example the rows that belong to the gap-analysis lanes L0-L9 of the sample policy document build plan (read `1_Project_Definition/2026-10-20_SRO-VIC_BUILD_PLAN.md` for the lane names, its filename stays out of Jira) become ONE epic. At most 4 epics, each with at least 3 member rows; the grouping rule and every row's epic are written to the seed log BEFORE the first create. A row that fits no group has no epic. No "miscellaneous" epic.
3. **One issue per SEED row.** Type Task (Bug only if the row is plainly a defect; say which rows). **Summary** = the row's item text, first sentence, markdown stripped, at most 200 characters (a cut gets an ellipsis; list the cut rows). **Description** (Atlassian document format) = line 1 `Backlog row #<N>.`, line 2 `State: <the State cell text>` (stripped of markdown), then the item text in full. Sanitise the TEXT (not the sources): see the Jira text rule below; record every edited token (row, old, new) in the seed log. Never invent content. **Labels:** `composer-seed-1023` on all; plus `needs-kam` on rows whose State is the project owner's to act on: State opens with KAM'S CALL, or the State text names the owner's word, call, decision or send as the next step (print the matching phrase per row; READY FOR REVIEW rows whose next step is "Kam sends"/"Kam reviews" get it too); plus `plan-comp-<row#>` (the idempotency key). No other label.
4. **Idempotent seeding (the MPS lesson).** Before the first create and before EACH create run `project = <KEY> AND labels = "plan-comp-<row#>"` (JQL always begins `project = <KEY> AND`, via `GET /rest/api/3/search/jql`): 0 expected; if 1, it exists (a timed-out call) and is not created again. Controls before the first write: `project = <KEY> AND text ~ "zzqxvbw"` 0; `project = <KEY> AND labels = "composer-seed-1023"` 0; a search for a label you know exists in the seed log after the first create returns 1. After the seed: every issue read back equal (type, summary, description text, labels, epic parent) to the log; 0 missing, 0 duplicates; one-character mutant of a summary compares unequal. Seed order = row number ascending (epics first). Keys are not contiguous-assumed: the log maps row# to key as returned.
5. **Seed log:** `1_Project_Definition/Registers/2026-10-23_jira-seed-log.md` (and a `.json` twin): the project facts (key, id, board id, lead accountId only as an opaque id), the classification table, the grouping rule, row# to key for every created issue, the edited-token list, the AMBIGUOUS list. Not a secret in it.

**Jira text rule (binding for every Jira summary, description, label and comment you write).** No person's name (the owner, the coordinators, the reviewer, anyone), no client or sample-client name or abbreviation (the sample policy document's title, its client's name and any abbreviation of either; say "the sample policy document"; also HP-family names, partner names, the sample engagement customers), no decision-card id (any `composer-...` or `*-1014`-style card slug), no price, no credential. Branch names, PR numbers, sha, file paths and the project's own C-/B-/row ids are allowed. The ONLY exception is the label `needs-kam` (specified by the owner; it is a label, not text). Edit the token (neutral phrase) and log the edit; if a row cannot be made neutral without losing meaning, seed it with the unchanged meaning in neutral words and list it under EDITED.
**Closing check (program, printed):** build the term list from what you actually find in the SEED rows' text (every capitalised person-like word you see is a candidate) plus: Kam, Kamil, Kreiser, Paul, Friday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Synnex, HP, HPSM, "Solutions Centre", SRO, "SRO_VIC", VIC, Quandarra, Yarrowind, Wombalong, Tarnwick, and the card pattern `composer-`. (a) Local: scan the exact payloads you sent: 0 hits (control: a planted term in a scratch payload is found). (b) Live: for each term `project = <KEY> AND text ~ "<term>"` returns **0**; control that can fail: `project = <KEY> AND text ~ "composer"` (the project's own word) returns more than 0 and `text ~ "zzqxvbw"` returns 0. `text ~` does not index labels, so `needs-kam` does not trip it; check labels in (a) with the one exception coded. Any hit: edit that issue's text (a Jira write inside your project, allowed), re-read, re-run, record.

**Step 5. Records (from your own worktree; nothing in the dirty root is staged).**
